# Couplings that survive a fusion, beside the ceiling the part's share puts on them. One row per
# upper coupling that has a lower coupling underneath it between the same two layers, carrying
# what the upper filing claims and what the lower one can support.
#
# A coupling propagates upward and attenuates
#   If layer A moves with layer B, and A is fused into a bigger layer one level up, the dependence
#   survives but weakens, because the composed layer is only partly A. At most A's share of the
#   composed layer can move, so the upper strength is bounded by the lower strength times that
#   share.
#
# The share is the nameplate's, read where it is largest
#   Relief acts on a constraint, and a layer's constraint is its supply, so A's share of X is A's
#   nameplate, converted into X's unit by the part's factor, over X's filed nameplate. The ceiling
#   accuses when it is exceeded, so the share is read at its largest: A's high at the factor's
#   high, over X's low. That may pair corners no single week reaches, and it errs toward not
#   accusing, which is the direction a bound has to err. Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry
#   `coupling_attenuates`.
#
# Not derivable, which is why it is checked
#   A filer could put any number in the upper document; the ceiling comes from two other
#   documents, which is a comparison no single-document validator can make.
#
# Seven joins, each narrowing the population
#   The upper coupling, the part carrying its `from` layer, that part's document, the lower
#   coupling, the part carrying the `to` layer, and the two nameplates the share is computed from. A
#   coverage table that reproduces only the first two counts more rows than the rule examines, and
#   can report a thin rule as well covered. That is the trap this repository is written to name,
#   firing inside its own coverage table, and it is why nothing counts a population except by
#   composing the population.
-- pm:Coupling at two levels, related through asrt:Fusion/asrt:Part, capped by the part's nameplate share.
SELECT x.upper_filing, x.from_layer, x.to_layer, x.lower_filing, x.share,
       x.lo_low  * x.share AS ceil_low,
       x.lo_mode * x.share AS ceil_mode,
       x.lo_high * x.share AS ceil_high,
       x.low, x.mode, x.high
FROM (
    SELECT up.filing AS upper_filing, up.from_layer, up.to_layer,
           lo.filing AS lower_filing,
           lo.low AS lo_low, lo.mode AS lo_mode, lo.high AS lo_high,
           pn.n_high * coalesce(pf.factor_high, 1) / cn.n_low AS share,
           up.low, up.mode, up.high
    FROM      (
        :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
    ) up
    JOIN      (
        :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
    ) pf
           ON pf.composition = up.filing AND pf.composed_layer = up.from_layer
    JOIN      (
        :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
    ) lo
           ON lo.filing = pf.part_filing AND lo.from_layer = pf.part_layer
    JOIN      (
        :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
    ) pt
           ON pt.composition = up.filing AND pt.composed_layer = up.to_layer
          AND pt.part_layer = lo.to_layer
    JOIN      (
        :compose(layers/nameplate.sqlc)
    ) pn
           ON pn.filing = pf.part_filing AND pn.layer = pf.part_layer
    JOIN      (
        :compose(layers/nameplate.sqlc)
    ) cn
           ON cn.filing = up.filing      AND cn.layer = up.from_layer
    WHERE up.mode IS NOT NULL AND lo.mode IS NOT NULL
      AND pf.factor_state IN ('omitted', 'stated')
      AND cn.n_low > 0
) x
